WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.440
Late last year, Alibaba Co-founder Jack Ma
was confirmed as an official member of the

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.830
Chinese Communist Party.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:11.810
Jack Ma also happens to have a not-very-communist-sounding
estimated net worth that peaked at $50 billion

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.849
dollars in 2018.

00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:17.680
Even though China remains officially committed
to a communist future – with equality for

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.890
all and wealth distribution based on need
– its history of capitalistic compromise

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.310
has made it *the* place to turn personal millions
in billions.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:30.140
This is your Bloomberg QuickTake on China’s
Billionaires.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.450
By one estimate, China creates a new billionaire
every two days.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:39.120
The seeds of the billionaire boom were planted
four decades ago, beginning in 1978, when

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.819
communist party patriarch Deng Xiaoping opened
the country up to private enterprise and investment.

00:00:44.819 --> 00:00:49.809
Since then, China began shifting away from
agriculture, and increased its focus on manufacturing,

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:53.269
eventually leading to growth that turned the
country into the world’s second-largest

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:55.679
economy, behind the U.S.

00:00:55.679 --> 00:01:00.039
There's been this discussion of what is socialism,
what is capitalism, and Deng just kind of

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:02.429
said, "What they're not is, they're not poverty."

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:06.860
The lines between business and politics have
become increasingly hazy as President Xi Jinping

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.220
has led a campaign to ensure the communist
party plays a leading role in all aspects

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.250
of society.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:18.140
the internet companies have been really this
sort of shining example of what China hopes

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:25.430
it can become, which is cutting edge, smart
people doing cool things, and making products

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:26.940
that people around the world want to use.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:33.550
I think they're a lot less enthused with massive
amounts of private wealth that that often

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:34.550
creates.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.790
But China hasn’t given up on Communism,
it just made full communism a goal with no

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:39.790
time frame.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:43.900
On the roadmap to Communism, the country will
have to become fully socialist first, which

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.480
Xi has set a goal of reaching by 2035.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.320
China is a communist country, but it's a one-party
state, so in that sense, the communist party

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:59.001
is very intensely focused on making sure it
can maintain control and stability, at the

00:01:59.001 --> 00:02:06.610
expense of a lot of civil rights and sort
of other freedoms and liberties that other

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.690
countries sort of take for granted sometimes.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:12.880
Jack Ma was among 100 people honored by the
Communist Party as part of the celebration

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.890
marking 40 years of China’s economic reform
- evidence that on the issue of communism,

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.420
China is more than content to wait.

